solnix: tzmon -- enable the ThinkPad HKEY event interface before reading THM0.

SYMPTOM (ThinkPad X1 Extreme Gen5, 21DE): the machine stops at "Press any key
to reboot." from seconds to minutes after boot, in multi-user and in boot -s.
The console line just before the prompt (photographed 2026-10-17):

  tzmon: WARNING: tzmon: Thermal zone (\_TZ_.THM0) is critically hot (128 C);
  initiating rapid shutdown

ROOT CAUSE: tzmon's _CRT handling calls mdboot(A_REBOOT, AD_HALT) when _TMP >=
_CRT. In the ThinkPad DSDTs read for this (P1 Gen 4i 20Y4 and X1 Carbon Gen 11
21HN, from linuxhw/ACPI; the X1E Gen5's own tables are not dumped yet, but 128 C
is exactly TCRT, which no real sensor reports), \_TZ.THM0._TMP returns TCRT
whenever the EC sets its TSL2 "sensor critically hot" level while HKEY.DHKC is
0, that is while no OS driver has enabled the hotkey/event interface. The Linux
thinkpad_acpi driver sets DHKC with HKEY.MHKC(1) at load; illumos has no
ThinkPad driver, so DHKC stays 0 and the first TSL2 event halts the system.
With DHKC=1 the firmware reports TSL2 as HKEY event 0x6022 instead (Linux logs
"THERMAL EMERGENCY: a sensor reports something is extremely hot" and keeps
running) and _TMP returns the real temperature. _TMP still returns TCRT for
TSL3, the EC's shutdown level, regardless of DHKC, so tzmon still halts there,
and the EC's own thermal protection is unchanged.

FIX: in tzmon_attach, before the zones are enumerated, find the HKEY device
(_HID LEN0268/LEN0068/IBM0068), install a notify handler that drains MHKP and
logs the thermal alarm events (0x6011/0x6012/0x6021/0x6022) with the EC's
TSL0-TSL3 sensor bitmaps, then call MHKC(1). Detach and the attach failure
paths call MHKC(0) and remove the handler. Without an HKEY device (every
non-ThinkPad) nothing changes. Side effect, as under Linux: with DHKC=1 the
firmware routes hotkeys such as Fn+F4 (sleep) to HKEY events instead of the
SLPB notify; the handler ignores them.

